"2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}-3y=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+bx{y}^{′}+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y={x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-64781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x{y}^{″}-2y=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y={x}^{3}{e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+4{x}^{2}y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+xy=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y={x}^{3}{e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+4x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+4\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda +2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-64791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}x"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+4x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+4\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda -1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-64896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/4}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/4}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3/2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1/2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(4\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-93849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-4}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/4}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{3/4}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-2}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93849" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1/4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-3y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+7x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda -3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(2\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-93850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5/2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1/2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-64803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-2}"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=8,\\,t>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =3.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(2)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0=2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=8.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}(2)^{2} \\\\ 8 & ={c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =-4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =8{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{3}-4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-64897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(t)=2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "y(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t"] ["math" "y(t)=4{t}^{3}-t"] ["math" "y(t)={t}^{2}-4t"] ["math" "y(t)={t}^{3}-4t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-64897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(t)={t}^{3}-4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{x},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{c}_{1}x+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ and ${c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1-{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{3{c}_{1}+1}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =3\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-93855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}"] ["math" "y(x)=-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}"] ["math" "y(x)=2{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}"] ["math" "y(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+4\\sqrt{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=3{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,t>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=1-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}\\sqrt{t}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & ={c}_{1}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 4 & =1-{c}_{2}+\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{{c}_{2}+2}{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =6\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=6\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-5t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-93856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(t)=-4t-2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}"] ["math" "y(t)=-5t-3\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}"] ["math" "y(t)=2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-t"] ["math" "y(t)=2t-3\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}"] ["math" "y(t)=6\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-5t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(t)=6\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}-5t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{2}}+{c}_{2}x,\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(2)=1.$ Substituting $y(2)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{4}+2{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=4-8{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=2.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{{x}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=2$ and ${c}_{1}=4-8{c}_{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-\\frac{8-16{c}_{2}}{8}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & =3{c}_{2}-1 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-93858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=2x-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y(x)=3x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y(x)=4x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3x"] ["math" "y(x)=x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=x-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{1/3}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+7x{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{x}^{2/3}}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5{c}_{1}}{6} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{5}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c33e9d361f86a177eada147a8bbd3288a806c3608fa1d1927915ee889055709f"} {"question" "q-93862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=3{x}^{1/3}+\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x}}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{1/3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}+\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{x}}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{1/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "q-93862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{1/3}}{5}-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{x}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/state.json" "file_sha256" "a557ea75dd372892f59ea080829a1ea27f0f96af0e30a81e4e7fede7c2353661"} {"question" "e-6624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n- $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n\n- $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n\n- $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/example-6624.json" "file_sha256" "29930103f2b4c0643aeadfef67aa67d098f521de50f4dfa5fec647b56a44b96a"} {"question" "e-6624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2,$ $b=6,$ $c=-5,$ and $f(x)=0.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2.$ Instead, it is multiplied by an $x.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/example-6624.json" "file_sha256" "29930103f2b4c0643aeadfef67aa67d098f521de50f4dfa5fec647b56a44b96a"} {"question" "e-6625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/example-6625.json" "file_sha256" "097990480e07e3cfe99a3ae9f1b76220b4ebf1456bd27b45eaf18eccf9f309c0"} {"question" "e-6625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+8t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+8\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ 3{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(3\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -2$ and $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={c}_{1}{t}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/example-6625.json" "file_sha256" "097990480e07e3cfe99a3ae9f1b76220b4ebf1456bd27b45eaf18eccf9f309c0"} {"question" "e-6626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/example-6626.json" "file_sha256" "e70134627fe7243144f1c0c874cedd96b1af3a8c667285574f562766f477204b"} {"question" "e-6626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad  c_2 = -c_1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{4}}+2{c}_{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}) \\\\ 5 & =-5{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069407/example-6626.json" "file_sha256" "e70134627fe7243144f1c0c874cedd96b1af3a8c667285574f562766f477204b"}] "usage" {"q-64714" [] "e-6626" [] "e-6624" [] "e-6625" [] "q-64803" []} "basis" 2369 "retractions" [[17592186148137 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 2y = 0, t > 0$."] [17592186147293 :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n1. $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n2. $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n3. $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$"] [17592186147293 :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2$, $b=6$, $c=-5$, and $f(x)=0$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2$. Instead, it is multiplied by an $x$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186147294 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0$."] [17592186147294 :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3t^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 8t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n3λ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 8λt^{λ} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n3λ^{2} + 5λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)(3λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= c_{1}t^{-2} + c_{2}t^{1/3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{t^{2}} + c_{2}t^{1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147295 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2}$,\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0$."] [17592186147295 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad c_2 = -c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|=-3\\frac{c_{1}}{x^{4}} + 2c_{2}x$.\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n$5|=-3c_{1} + 2c_{2}; 5|=-3c_{1} + 2(-c_{1}); 5|=-5c_{1}; c_{1}|=-1⇒c_{2} = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146810 17592186146811 17592186146812 17592186146813 17592186146814 17592186146834 17592186146835 17592186146836 17592186146837 17592186146838] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146809 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146809 :answer-field/id] [17592186146809 :answer-field/key] [17592186146809 :answer-field/type] [17592186146833 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146833 :answer-field/id] [17592186146833 :answer-field/key] [17592186146833 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
